      "text": "ment, but it has seen a rapid development in recent years. This treatment method is based on utilizing the property of PFAS molecules having both hydrophilic and hydrophobic components that leads to the concentra- tion of PFAS on the water-air interface. In foam fractionation, PFAS are concentrated in the foam, which forms on the water surface due to provided aeration. The foam is collected by overflow or by suction. The process is most often performed in batch mode ( Lee and Venkatesan, 2024 , Wang et al., 2023 ), although a continuous process is also possible ( Smith et al., 2022 ). The removed collapsed foam (foamate) is further concentrated by subsequent foam fractionation steps ( Burns et al., 2022 ) or treated by electrochemical oxidation ( Smith et al., 2023b ). Treatment of landfill leachate (Malovanyy et al., 2024), nanofiltration concentrate produced during drinking water treatment ( McCleaf et al., 2023 ), and deionized water spiked with PFAS ( Buckley et al., 2023 ) was studied in the majority of the published studies. Only two studies, with one experiment in each study, investigated the removal of PFAS from municipal wastewater by foam fractionation ( Baresel et al., 2022 ; Wang et al., 2023 ). Moreover, Nguyen et al."
